bitcoin's price is determined by supply  and demand just as the price of any  financial asset but what we know is that  bitcoin is a fixed supply asset as a  programmatic monetary policy meaning  that you don't actually have to  calculate what the supply potentially  will be in the future no we know with  100 certainty exactly what the supply is  today how much bitcoins coming into the  market and also what the future monetary  policy decisions will be it's all  written in code that can be analyzed by  anyone in the world and so ultimately if  you're looking at price all you have to  do is you have to forecast what demand  will be demand rises for a fixed supply  asset price goes up if demand falls  price goes down but one other nuance of  the evaluation of bitcoin's price is how  much of the bitcoin is actually  available in the supply for people to  come and purchase what i mean is that if  there's lots of bitcoin actually sitting  on exchanges then of course anyone could  turn around and with a click of a button  sell it into the market that's the  potential for a lot of cell pressure the  potential for the to push the price down  but if bitcoin's coming off exchanges it  means that people have gone they've  purchased bitcoin and they're taking it  into cold storage or taking self-custody  of it there's more friction for them to  go onto the exchange and sell it it  usually is a bullish sign and so what  we've seen over the last nine or 10 days  or so is the fact that about 54 000  bitcoin are actually coming off of  exchanges the beauty of bitcoin is that  we can literally see on the blockchain  that 54 000 bitcoin or so have been  taken off the exchanges this is the  balance across all exchanges for bitcoin  and as you watch this occur you can see  over the long term trend we continue to  see more and more bitcoin actually being  removed from these exchanges so if we  then take a look at the long-term trend  what we can see going all the way back  to january 20